+# confkit: in-repo parser replaces `inilite`
+
+## What changed
+
+- `confkit/parse.py`: rewritten. The `inilite` import is gone. `loads(text, base_dir=None, source="<string>")` and `load(path)` now implement `docs/FORMAT.md`: BOM handling, LF/CRLF/CR line endings, comments, dotted sections and keys, quoted strings with escapes, multi-line values, lists, typed scalars, `@include` with cycle detection and per-file sources, and every error message from the spec with its line number. `ParseError` (in `confkit/errors.py`, unchanged) is raised with the exact `str()` format.
+- `tests/test_format.py`: new file, 50 tests covering the spec sections above and the CLI exit codes. No existing test was edited.
+- `vendor/`, `docs/`, `README.md`, `confkit/__main__.py`, `confkit/errors.py` and `confkit/__init__.py` are untouched. No dependencies were added.
+
+## What I verified
+
+- `python -m unittest`: before the change, 2 errors at import (`No module named 'inilite'`). After the change: `Ran 63 tests ... OK` (the 13 original tests plus 50 new ones).
+- `python -m confkit` with no arguments: prints the usage line, exit 2.
+- `python -m confkit FILE`: valid file prints JSON. `--get server.port` prints `9000`, exit 0. `--get server.nope` prints `no such key: server.nope` to stderr, exit 2. A file with `a = 1` then `a = 2` prints `FILE:2: duplicate key: a` to stderr, exit 1.
+- `grep -rn "inilite\|vendor" confkit tests`: no matches.
+- Python used: 3.14.3 only.
+
+## Not verified / uncertain
+
+- Only Python 3.14 was tested. No other version was run.
+- The expected values in the tests were written by me from `docs/FORMAT.md`. There is no independent reference implementation, so a misreading of the spec would be shared by code and tests.
+- Spec points where I chose a reading:
+  - An unquoted list token may contain spaces, so `[1 2]` is `["1 2"]`. I read the token rule literally; the spec's "two items without a comma" could mean this is an error.
+  - A quoted list item followed by something other than `,` or `]` (for example `["a"b]` or `["x" "y"]`) is `invalid list`, not `unexpected text after string`.
+  - A backslash at the end of a line inside a double-quoted string is `unterminated string`, not `invalid escape`.
+  - `@include"x"` (no space) is accepted. `@include.x` is `invalid line` because `.` continues the word.
+  - `\uD800`-style surrogate escapes are accepted and produce a lone surrogate.
+- Not covered by the spec, so unchanged or left as-is:
+  - A root file that is not valid UTF-8 raises `UnicodeDecodeError`, not `ParseError`. An undecodable *include* is `cannot read include`.
+  - `python -m confkit MISSING.ini` still shows a traceback for `OSError`.
+  - Decimal integers longer than Python's int-digit limit (4300 digits on 3.14) will raise `ValueError` from `int()`. I did not test this.
+- The workspace guard blocked one scratch file placed next to the repo, so scratch files went to `/tmp/confkit-scratch/` outside the repo. Nothing there is part of the change.
